id: k8s.deployment
kind: kubernetes
title: Deployment
aliases: deploy, workload controller
tags: workloads, availability
questions:
  - how do you run a stateless service with N replicas?
invariant: The replica count is declared; the Deployment controller converges Pods to it via ReplicaSets.
answer_30s: Declare replicas and a Pod template; the Deployment controller keeps exactly that many Pods running and moves between template versions by creating ReplicaSets.
answer_2m: |
  A Deployment owns ReplicaSets, and a ReplicaSet owns Pods. Changing the Pod
  template does not edit Pods: it creates a new ReplicaSet and shifts replicas
  from the old one to the new one under surge/unavailable bounds. Self-healing
  is the same loop at rest: a deleted Pod is recreated to match the declared
  count. It is the default controller for stateless workloads.
when_to_use:
  - stateless services
  - gradual application rollout
avoid_when:
  - stable identity per replica is required (use StatefulSet)
  - every node needs exactly one copy (use DaemonSet)
commands:
  - kubectl get deployments -A => which workloads exist and how many are ready
  - kubectl rollout status deployment/api => is a rollout still in progress or stuck
  - kubectl describe deployment/api => which ReplicaSet is current and what the strategy is
minimal_example: |
  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: Deployment
  metadata: { name: api }
  spec:
    replicas: 3
    selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }
    template:
      metadata: { labels: { app: api } }
      spec:
        containers:
          - name: api
            image: api:1.2.3
            readinessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 } }
failure_modes:
  - selector and template labels do not match
  - image tag never changes, so no rollout happens
  - no readiness probe, so broken Pods receive traffic
follow_ups:
  - what exactly happens to the old ReplicaSet after a rollout?
related:
  - k8s.replica-set
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update
  - k8s.probe.readiness
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/
